[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Tuesday November 1st I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  self-driving Trucking Company too simple  has been grappling with a number of  challenges in recent months  its stock price is down 90 for the year  and according to exclusive wsj reporting  the FBI Securities and Exchange  Commission and the committee on foreign  investment in the U.S are looking into  whether the company improperly financed  and transferred technology to a Chinese  startup on Monday a day after the Wall  Street Journal reported on the  investigations into the company the  board of too simple announced it had  fired its chief executive and co-founder  shouty Howe so what happens next for too  simple which had been seen as the leader  in the self-driving Trucking sector  joining us to discuss this is our  venture capital and startups reporter  Heather Somerville hi Heather thanks for  coming back on the show Hey Zoe Heather  it feels like there's a lot of moving  parts to this story so I just want to  take a second to break some of those  down there are two companies that are at  the heart of this too simple and a  Chinese startup called hydron what are  the connections between the two  the connections are in the founders of  these two companies so too simple was  founded in 2015 by mochen and shouty  Howe they have run the company for the  most part these last several years in  2021 this other company emerged called  hydron which says it is developing  hydrogen-powered self-driving trucks and  has most of its operations in China and  the founder and head of it is mochen who  as I mentioned is also the co-founder of  too simple  in addition to hydron has said that too  simple is its first and so far as we can  tell only customer and that too simple  has pledged to buy hundreds of trucks  from hydron that it will equip with  self-driving technology  and one more thread I'll throw in there  is they share an investor a really  important investor his name is Charles  Chao he's the chief executive and  chairman of a big Chinese conglomerate  called Cena Corp and he was a critical  backer of too simple and then of hydron  and really the relationship just builds  from there okay so according to people  familiar with the matter there are  government probes into too simple and  this relationship with Hydro has a lot  to do with it can you just break down  what those are yeah Zoe there's a lot  going on here and so there are  investigations by government authorities  from the FBI the SEC and the committee  on foreign investment in the United  States which is often called cypheus in  addition the board of directors at too  simple open an investigation  in July into what too simple was doing  regarding hydron and so we've had these  four concurrent investigations all of  which are ongoing okay so what are they  looking at primarily government  authorities are looking at whether too  simple improperly financed hydron and  transferred technology to hydron and  they are looking at it from the  perspective of did too simple fail to  make the Securities filings and this  disclosures that are required of them  whenever they have a so-called related  party transaction  so it's really being looked at it as a  potential Financial crime and the FBI in  particular is concerned about  shareholders into simple being defrauded  if shouty how and others are too simple  willingly gave technology to a Chinese  competitor because obviously that  technology and the proprietary nature of  that technology is what makes or made  too simple so valuable to them  companies that about these  investigations well the company told us  that they were not aware of any FBI or  SEC investigations the company said it's  been in discussions with cypheus it's  also notable that cepheus had another  investigation into two simple last year  and it has been in communication with  two simple since then and so too simple  said it has discussions with cepheus but  it declined to comment on the substance  of those discussions and it declined to  comment on the board investigation now  we know the board investigation LED them  to fire the CEO which they announced on  Monday he was also their Chief  technology officer the president and the  chairman of the board what did the  company say about why it took that step  they said that they fired him because  what they had found so far in their  ongoing investigation  was that too simple had shared  confidential information with hydron and  the board didn't know about this  incident and it had shared confidential  information with hydron prior to  entering into a business deal with  hydron  in addition the Securities filing said  that the board discovered that several  two simple employees had worked for  hydron and gotten paid for their work  while also being on the payroll at too  simple and I will say that shouty posted  on LinkedIn a lengthy statement in which  he says that he denies any suggestion of  wrongdoing he has nothing to hide and he  stands by all of his actions and  decisions regarding too simple is isn't  there a bigger picture about competition  between the US and China here yeah  that's really the key backdrop to the  Zoe the United States and China have  both named the technology that underpins  self-driving Vehicles which is  artificial intelligence as critical  technology to National Security for  military purposes as well as for  economic competitiveness in September  President Biden issued an executive  order which was aimed at improving the  scrutiny of foreign investment in U.S  companies building critical technology  it also specifies that the technology  for hydrogen-powered vehicles which is  what hydron claims it's making is key to  the United States national security so  you have this issue where both China and  the US have openly declared repeatedly  that this technology is essential to  them both are working like crazy to  develop it and now we have a situation  where the company that had been  considered the lead and self-driving  Trucking  has a very questionable future we don't  really know what's going to happen next  to this company that has already been  through a lot of turmoil and now faces  what is probably the biggest challenge  of its existence all right I guess we'll  just have to wait and see a little bit  that's our reporter Heather Somerville  thanks for joining us Heather thanks Zoe  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  foreign

